# Accessibility Best Practices

## Image Accessibility
- Always include proper `alt` attributes for images
- Use descriptive alt text that conveys meaning
- Use Next.js `Image` component for optimization

## Semantic HTML
- Use semantic HTML elements (`header`, `main`, `section`, `article`, `footer`)
- Ensure proper heading hierarchy (h1, h2, h3, etc.)
- Use appropriate form elements and labels

## Interactive Elements
- Include `aria-label` for interactive elements without visible text
- Use `sr-only` class for screen reader only content
- Ensure keyboard navigation works properly
- Use proper focus management

## Dialog Accessibility
- Always include `DialogTitle` for screen readers
- Use `sr-only` class when title is visually hidden
- Ensure proper focus trapping

## Examples
```tsx
// Proper image with alt text
<Image
  src="/volunteers.jpg"
  alt="Horsforth in Bloom volunteers working in the community"
  width={600}
  height={400}
  className="rounded-lg shadow-lg"
  priority
/>

// Accessible dialog
<DialogContent>
  <DialogTitle className="sr-only">
    Image Gallery for {siteName}
  </DialogTitle>
  {/* Content */}
</DialogContent>

// Proper button with aria-label
<button
  aria-label="Close dialog"
  className="absolute top-4 right-4"
>
  <X className="h-4 w-4" />
</button>
```

## Color Contrast
- Ensure sufficient color contrast ratios
- Don't rely solely on color to convey information
- Test with screen readers and keyboard navigation